      "card_ids": ["C10"],
      "claim": "In dogs, IV-administered 5-MTHF rapidly equilibrated into CSF within 3 h, demonstrating meaningful CNS access after systemic administration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "N5-methyl tetrahydrofolate was recovered in dog CSF after IV administration against a concentration gradient, indicating specific transport across the BBB.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "In nonhuman primates, raltitrexed CSF penetration was limited (0.6–2.0%) and only detectable at a high dose, arguing for poor CNS access under normal barrier conditions.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The most direct and condition-relevant evidence from similar folates shows transporter-mediated CSF/brain entry after systemic dosing, and the disrupted-barrier condition would further favor meaningful CNS access. Negative normal-barrier records from antifolates and radiotracers are less decisive under barrier disruption.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct disrupted-barrier experiments are present in the retrieved evidence; normal-barrier outcomes were extrapolated.",
    "Similarity scores are modest (0.24–0.36) and the query structure is not disclosed, limiting transferability.",
    "Some similar molecules show poor normal-barrier CNS penetration, leaving residual uncertainty."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
